    'Unlike the Page’s fish, which rises into view, the contents of the Queen’s vessel are not simply exposed for inspection. The object’s complexity slows the eye. This gives contemplation a different texture: the viewer cannot immediately explain what the Queen sees, so her attention remains partly private. That privacy is a feature of the composition, not evidence that she possesses an objectively verified supernatural faculty unavailable to ordinary people.',
    'The shore also distinguishes her from the King seated amid a more unsettled expanse. Both belong to water-associated court scenes, but their relationships to that setting differ. The Queen’s image allows closeness and observation at an edge, where one environment meets another. A modern discussion of listening can grow from that arrangement: attention involves proximity without necessarily becoming identical with what is observed. Waite’s insistence on action then prevents the scene from being reduced to feeling everything while doing nothing.',
